\pard\b\fs22 Mask Tweaks\b0\fs20\par

\pard\sl287\slmult0\par

\pard\ri571\sl255\slmult0\fs22 A couple of parameters for tweaking the intersection blending can be found here. \ul Intersection Scale\ulnone  is especially important, this one should be synced with the Intersection Scale parameter of the Terrain shader to achieve perfect tiling.\fs20\par

\pard\sl268\slmult0\par

\pard\ri411\sl237\slmult0\ul\fs22 Mask Multiplier\ulnone  can be used to decrease/increase the alpha channel's influence on the mask and \ul Mask Power\ulnone  can soften/harden the edges of the mask.\par
\par
\ul Y Offset\ulnone  can be used to offset the height of the effect in a single parameter. Positions in negative Y-Position are now possible, too.\fs20\par

\pard\sl241\slmult0\par

\pard\b\fs22 Vertex Modification\b0\fs20\par

\pard\sl284\slmult0\par

\pard\ri91\sl254\slmult0\fs22 The two settings found here are \ul Target Normal\ulnone  and \ul Inflate Ground\ulnone . You can use Target Normal's XYZ to bend the Normals in the intersecting area to the set direction. The last component (W) controls the Intensity of the Normal Blending and works in a 0 - 1 range.\fs20\par

\pard\fs22\par

\pard\sl256\slmult0\ul Inflate Ground\ulnone  will push the object's vertices along the normal by the set value. You can use this function to make assets appear more sunken into the ground. It will automatically only affect vertices in the intersection area.\fs20\par

\pard\sl222\slmult0\par

\pard\b\fs22 Snow Settings\b0\fs20\par

\pard\sl284\slmult0\par

\pard\ri260\sl254\slmult0\fs22 I called this "Snow" since it behaves similar to how snow behaves in real life. It adds on top of your objects. You can check \ul Add Snow\ulnone  to enable/disable it and use \ul Snow Mask Multiplier\ulnone  to decrease/increase the effect, as well as \ul Snow Mask Power\ulnone  to create softer/harder edges.\fs20\par

\pard\sl337\slmult0\par

\pard\b\fs22 Vertex Color Settings\b0\fs20\par

\pard\sl359\slmult0\par

\pard\ri500\sl261\slmult0\fs22 Vertex Colors can be used to additionally tweak the blending. This way you can create per-asset variations from a single models and get more control artistic over the blending. You can use any vertex painter of your choice or apply the vertex colors inside your modeling program. Vertex colors channels are used in the following way:\fs20\par

\pard\sl19\slmult0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-358\li400\tx400\fs22 The red channel adds to the intersection blending mask.\par

\pard\sl41\slmult0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-358\li400\tx400 The green channel subtracts from the intersection blending mask.\par

\pard\sl41\slmult0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-358\li400\tx400 The blue channel adds to the model inflation mask.\par

\pard\sl41\slmult0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-358\li400\tx400 The alpha channel subtracts from the model inflation mask.\par

\pard\sl247\slmult0\fs20\par

\pard\cf3\b\f0\fs22 Standard Intersection Mask Terrain - Shader\cf0\b0\fs20\par

\pard\sl79\slmult0\f1\par

\pard\ri340\sl254\slmult0\fs22 This shader only has the most essential parameters exposed since most of it is managed by the Terrain system itself. The texture that uses the exposed parameters will automatically be the very first texture set in the Terrain component.\fs20\par

\pard\sl227\slmult0\par

\pard\b\fs22 Intersection Settings\b0\fs20\par

\pard\sl287\slmult0\par

\pard\ri60\sl237\slmult0\fs22 Here you can set the \ul Intersection Scale\ulnone  for the base terrain texture. Match it with the one set in your asset's shader to get perfect tiling.\fs20\par

\pard\sl241\slmult0\par

\pard\ul\fs22 Intersection Smoothness\ulnone  works like the PBR smoothness in Unity's Standard Shader.\fs20\par

\pard\sl200\slmult0\par

